JRBrown wrote:

> 2958. 	case HORN_OF_PLENTY:	/* not a musical instrument */
> 2959. 		if (obj->spe > 0) {

This checks to see if it has any charges.

> 2960. 		    struct obj *otmp;
> 2961. 		    const char *what;
> 2962.
> 2963. 		    consume_obj_charge(obj, TRUE);

Reduce the charges available.

> 2964. 		    if (!rn2(13)) {
> 2965. 			otmp = mkobj(POTION_CLASS, FALSE);

1-in-13 chance to make it a potion.

> 2966. 			if (objects[otmp->otyp].oc_magic) do {

Check it the potion is magic.  I haven't read the code for how many
types are.

> 2967. 			    otmp->otyp = rnd_class(POT_BOOZE, POT_WATER);

Make it into any type of potion in the range from booze to water.  To
know the possibilities you would need to read the table of objects.

> 2968. 			} while (otmp->otyp == POT_SICKNESS);

Loop as long as the result is sickness.

In other words try unlimited times for the potion to not be sickness.

> 2969. 			what = "A potion";
> 2970. 		    } else {
> 2971. 			otmp = mkobj(FOOD_CLASS, FALSE);

12-in-13 chance of a random food item.

> 2972. 			if (otmp->otyp == FOOD_RATION && !rn2(7))
> 2973. 			    otmp->otyp = LUMP_OF_ROYAL_JELLY;

Convert 1-in-7 regular food rations to royal jelly.  IIRC royal jelly
has no chance of appearing randomly so this gives it a chance.

> 2974. 			what = "Some food";
> 2975. 		    }
>
> As far as I can make out, this gives a 1-in-13 chance of making a
> random potion, then if the potion is magic it is converted into either
> booze, water, or sickness, with something that I don't understand
> controlling the choice of booze/water vs sickness. Since sickness can
> be converted to booze, but water can't (reliably), I'd like to figure
> out what this "if... do... while" thing does. Anyone able to explain
> it in small words for the coding-ignorant will earn my heartfelt
> gratitude.

The if is a decision point.  Do the rest if the test passes.

The do while is a loop.  Do the statements as long as the test passes.

If you're doing this for potions it seems like you will end up with a
ton of food in the process.


